Title: `gh auth token --secure-storage` ignores the config's active account and returns the globally-active keyring token

### Describe the bug

With two accounts on `github.com` kept in separate `GH_CONFIG_DIR`s, `gh auth token` returns the right token for each config, but `gh auth token --secure-storage` returns the token of whichever account was activated last globally, ignoring the active user in the config directory it was pointed at.

`--secure-storage` is a hidden flag, so it isn't something most people call directly. It matters because the `gh stack` extension shells out to exactly this command, so every `gh stack` call authenticates as the wrong account in a `GH_CONFIG_DIR` setup.

`gh` 2.98.0, also present on `main` as of v2.100.0. Linux (Fedora 44), keyring via libsecret.

### Steps to reproduce

Two accounts logged in on the same host, each in its own config dir, tokens in the keyring (no `oauth_token` in either `hosts.yml`):

```bash
gh auth login                                        # user-a -> ~/.config/gh
GH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-b gh auth login           # user-b -> ~/.config/gh-b
```

Each `hosts.yml` correctly records its own active user. The keyring holds three entries under service `gh:github.com`: `user-a`, `user-b`, and one with an empty username.

```bash
export GH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-b

gh auth status
#   Logged in to github.com account user-b (keyring)
#   Active account: true                                    <- correct

gh auth token
#   <user-b's token>                                        <- correct

gh auth token --secure-storage --hostname github.com
#   <user-a's token>                                        <- wrong

gh auth token --secure-storage --hostname github.com --user user-b
#   <user-b's token>                                        <- correct
```

I identified each token by SHA-256 fingerprint against the keyring entries, and on the wire with `GH_DEBUG=api`, where the `X-OAuth-Scopes` response header differs between the two accounts.

### Expected vs actual

|                                           | expected                      | actual                       |
| ----------------------------------------- | ----------------------------- | ---------------------------- |
| `gh auth token`                           | active user of the config dir | correct                      |
| `gh auth token --secure-storage`          | active user of the config dir | last globally activated user |
| `gh auth token --secure-storage --user X` | user X                        | correct                      |

### Cause

`tokenRun` resolves the active user in the non-secure branch but not in the secure one, [token.go#L72-L84](https://github.com/cli/cli/blob/a255baf71d13fe5947a4eb7ad521ffd412d64cee/pkg/cmd/auth/token/token.go#L72-L84):

```go
if opts.SecureStorage {
    if opts.Username == "" {
        val, _ = authCfg.TokenFromKeyring(hostname)          // no active-user resolution
    } else {
        val, _ = authCfg.TokenFromKeyringForUser(hostname, opts.Username)
    }
} else {
    if opts.Username == "" {
        val, _ = authCfg.ActiveToken(hostname)               // resolves active user
    } else {
        val, _, _ = authCfg.TokenForUser(hostname, opts.Username)
    }
}
```

`TokenFromKeyring` reads the unkeyed keyring slot, [config.go#L299](https://github.com/cli/cli/blob/a255baf71d13fe5947a4eb7ad521ffd412d64cee/internal/config/config.go#L299):

```go
func (c *AuthConfig) TokenFromKeyring(hostname string) (string, error) {
	return keyring.Get(keyringServiceName(hostname), "")
}
```

That slot is written by `activateUser` ([config.go#L460-L472](https://github.com/cli/cli/blob/a255baf71d13fe5947a4eb7ad521ffd412d64cee/internal/config/config.go#L460-L472)) on every `gh auth login` and `gh auth switch`. The OS keyring is a single store and isn't partitioned by `GH_CONFIG_DIR`, so that slot holds whichever account was activated last by any config dir, while each `hosts.yml` keeps its own `user:`. The two desync as soon as a second config dir is used.

`ActiveToken` gets this right: it resolves `ActiveUser(hostname)` from the config-scoped `hosts.yml`, calls `TokenFromKeyringForUser`, and treats the unkeyed slot only as a legacy fallback. `TokenFromKeyringForUser` also rejects a blank username, with a comment about the risk of returning the active token in surprising cases.

### Possible fix

Mirror what `ActiveToken` does, keeping the unkeyed slot as the fallback:

```go
if opts.SecureStorage {
    username := opts.Username
    if username == "" {
        username, _ = authCfg.ActiveUser(hostname)
    }
    if username == "" {
        val, _ = authCfg.TokenFromKeyring(hostname)
    } else if val, _ = authCfg.TokenFromKeyringForUser(hostname, username); val == "" {
        // Fall back to the legacy unkeyed slot, as ActiveToken does.
        val, _ = authCfg.TokenFromKeyring(hostname)
    }
}
```

I can put up a PR with tests if this looks like the right shape.

### Related issues

#12885 reports the non-secure path returning the wrong token under `GH_CONFIG_DIR`. That path was fixed in #11038 and behaves correctly for me on 2.98.0; this is the sibling branch that fix didn't cover. Also worth noting that on Linux/libsecret the unkeyed entry is a real distinct entry, so the wrong result is deterministic rather than ordering-dependent as described for macOS in #12885.

### Downstream effect

The `gh stack` extension resolves auth through go-gh, which reads only env vars and `oauth_token` in `hosts.yml`. With secure storage that finds nothing, so it shells out to `gh auth token --secure-storage --hostname github.com` (confirmed with a PATH shim; `GH_CONFIG_DIR` is inherited correctly) and gets the wrong token. The stacks endpoint 404s and the extension prints "Stacked PRs are not enabled for this repository", see github/gh-stack#394. In a `GH_CONFIG_DIR` setup `gh auth status` shows the correct account and `gh auth switch` isn't the fix, so that error is misleading here.
